 in a bill-of-materials analysis cited by MarketScale, that Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation, and that Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\",\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-6\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-2\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Nvidia Vera Rubin VR200 rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems; memory's share of the rack bill of materials rose from 5 to 10 percent on GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on VR200 while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\",\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-1\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-3\",\n    \"summary\": \"In the Morgan Stanley-derived analysis reported by MarketScale, first shipments of Nvidia's Vera Rubin racks are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter.\",\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-3\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-4\",\n    \"summary\": \"MarketScale reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand is the force behind the price increase in Nvidia's next rack and is now raising component costs across the market, that contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply with device makers including Samsung and Apple expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products, and that memory is a quarter or more of rack value so memory availability and lead times deserve the same planning attention as accelerator allocation.\",\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-1\",\n     \"clean-3\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-5\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler